s likely due to absence of fever (37.5°C is borderline) and lack of gallstone history", "Acute pyelonephritis: less likely due to lack of urinary symptoms (though urethritis history exists)", "Alcoholic hepatitis: unlikely in acute presentation with shock, though chronic alcohol use is noted"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension with tachycardia (shock pattern)", "Recent significant weight loss", "Unprotected sexual activity (risk for ectopic pregnancy)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Biliary Tract Obstruction with Complications", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.12887082855714574, "posterior": 0.07105956964473839, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Exercise-induced RUQ pain increase is a classic biliary colic trigger, moderately supporting biliary tract obstruction. However, absence of gallstone history w…"], "evidence_against": ["Borderline fever and no gallstone history weakly argue against biliary tract obstruction as the shock etiology."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic Vascular Compromise", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.01, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of neurological signs does not rule out hepatic vascular compromise, which remains a possible but less supported cause of shock."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Reproductive Tract Hemorrhage", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5213846116687921, "posterior": 0.5496719502732568, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Unprotected sexual activity increases suspicion for reproductive tract hemorrhage, but absence of vaginal bleeding or ovarian tenderness weakly reduces its lik…"], "evidence_against": ["Lack of direct hemorrhage signs weakly reduces probability of reproductive tract hemorrhage, though β-hCG pending maintains diagnostic relevance."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.9, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Surgical Abdomen (e.g., Perforated Viscus, Hemorrhage)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.011, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["No distension or bowel sound changes weakly argue against surgical abdomen (e.g., perforation), though acute hemorrhage remains possible."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Acute Pancreatitis with Systemic Inflammation", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Exercise-induced RUQ pain increase moderately supports common bile duct obstruction by stones (LR+ 6.33), though absence of gallstone history and jaundice weak…"]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Common Bile Duct Obstruction by Radio-Opaque Stones (Choledocholithiasis)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.061, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of gallstone history and jaundice weakly argues against common bile duct obstruction by stones (LR- 0.82), though exercise-induced RUQ pain moderately …"]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Acute Biliary Colic from Gallstones (Cholelithiasis)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of gallstone history weakly argues against acute biliary colic (LR- 0.82), though exercise-induced RUQ pain provides minimal support."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Biliary Stricture (Benign or Malignant) with Acute Exacerbation", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.01, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of jaundice/pale stools weakly argues against biliary stricture (LR- 0.82), though recent weight loss provides some support."]}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of vaginal bleeding or ovarian tenderness moderately argues against ectopic pregnancy (LR- 0.125), though β-hCG testing remains pending."]}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Ruptured Corpus Luteum Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.009, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B3.3": {"id": "B3.3", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with Secondary Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.54, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of gallstone history and lack of jaundice/dark urine argue against common bile duct obstruction by stones as the cause of shock?", "summary": "Absence of gallstone history and jaundice weakly argues against common bile duct obstruction by stones (LR- 0.82), though exercise-induced RUQ pain moderately supports it."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of gallstone history argue against acute biliary colic from gallstones as the cause of RUQ pain and shock?", "summary": "Absence of gallstone history weakly argues against acute biliary colic (LR- 0.82), though exercise-induced RUQ pain provides minimal support."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of jaundice, pale stools, or itching argue against biliary stricture as the cause of shock and RUQ pain?", "summary": "Absence of jaundice/pale stools weakly argues against biliary stricture (LR- 0.82), though recent weight loss provides some support."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of vaginal bleeding or ovarian tenderness on exam argue against ectopic pregnancy with hemorrhage as the cause of shock?", "summary": "Absence of vaginal bleeding or ovarian tenderness moderately argues against ectopic pregnancy (LR- 0.125), though β-hCG testing remains pending."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of neurological signs (e.g., abducens palsy, abnormal cranial nerve morphology) and typical features of hepatic vascular compromise (e.g., ascites, lower extremity edema) argue again…", "summary": "The absence of neurological signs (e.g., abducens palsy) and typical features of hepatic vascular compromise (e.g., ascites, lower extremity edema) weakly reduces the likelihood of hepatic vascular compromise as the cause of shock and RUQ pain."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the recent significant weight loss and absence of neurological signs (e.g., abducens palsy) argue for ovarian torsion with secondary hemorrhage as the cause of shock and RUQ pain?", "summary": "The recent significant weight loss and absence of neurological signs (e.g., abducens palsy) provide minimal support for ovarian torsion with secondary hemorrhage (B3.3) as a cause of shock and RUQ pain (LR+ 1.33). These findings do not significantly alter the probability of other branches but align…"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.327], ["B3.3", 0.281], ["B1", 0.245]]}, {"top": [["B3", 0.521], ["B3.3", 0.504], ["B1", 0.129]]}, {"top": [["B3", 0.55], ["B3.3", 0.54], ["B1", 0.071]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110/minute) with end-organ hypoperfusion (10/10 RUQ pain) indicate shock requiring immediate stabilization. High-probability time-critical candidates (B3.3 ovarian torsion with hemorrhage, posterior 0.54/danger 0.6) and pending β-hCG test for reproductive emergencies necessitate urgent intervention.", "required_actions": ["Initiate vasopressors if fluids fail to improve perfusion", "Obtain FAST ultrasound for intra-abdominal bleeding"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "[static mode override] Emergency override is active due to shock requiring immediate stabilization. Dangerous alternatives (B2: hepatic vascular compromise [danger 0.8/posterior 0.01], B1.1: common bile duct obstruction [danger 0.8/posterior 0.061]) have posterior <0.10. Required actions (FAST ultrasound, β-hCG) must be prioritized over further differential refinement. — emergency_override suppressed in static_diagnosis_qa mode"}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.7, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.5403426453784564, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Abdominal distention', 'Ovarian torsion', 'Hepatic encephalopathy', 'Ectopic pregnancy', 'Abdominal discomfort', 'Acute abdominal pain', 'Vaginal Bleeding', 'Hepatic vascular malformations'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.406}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B3.3", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with Secondary Hemorrhage", "posterior": 0.54, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=75%, source=both]\n\nHepatic Vascular Compromise vs Acute Surgical Abdomen (e.g., Perforated Viscus, Hemorrhage):\n  Favours first:  abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, absent bowel sounds, altered mental status, constipation, diarrhea\n\nHepatic Vascular Compromise vs Ovarian Torsion with Secondary Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, dizziness, fainting, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nAcute Surgical Abdomen (e.g., Perforated Viscus, Hemorrhage) vs Ovarian Torsion with Secondary Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  abdominal distension, absent bowel sounds, altered mental status, constipation, diarrhea\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, fainting, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, frequent urge to urinate\n\nRelated to Hepatic Vascular Compromise: budd-chiari syndrome, hepatic infarction, hepatic vein thrombosis\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → pain → Hepatic Vascular Compromise\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → shoulder pain → Hepatic Vascular Compromise\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → pain → Hepatic Vascular Compromise"}
